Here is the full **testsmells.org 19-smell academic audit** of `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`, with integration-test calibration applied (the class carries `[TestCategory("Integration")]`).

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH 1 — Sleepy Test

**Affected:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000); // blocks thread for 3 seconds
```

Even in integration tests, a hard `Thread.Sleep` is a smell — it introduces non-deterministic timing and slows the suite. Worse, the method is `async Task` yet uses a synchronous blocking call instead of `await Task.Delay`.

**Fix — use a polling retry loop:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll with timeout instead of sleeping
    var timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
    var interval = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(200);
    var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();
    while (sw.Elapsed < timeout)
    {
        if (_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
            break;
        await Task.Delay(interval);
    }

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        "Notification was not sent within the timeout period.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** 3-second unconditional wait per run; false failures if the system is slow; false passes if the system takes > 3 s.

---

### 🔴 HIGH 2 — Conditional Test Logic

**Affected:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101-108)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

The `if`/`else` branch means **one assertion path never executes**. If the data always creates a regular `User`, the `PremiumUser` branch is dead code giving false coverage. If it returns a `PremiumUser`, the name check is dead.

**Fix — split into two explicit tests:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_RegularUser_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    var user = new User("eve@example.com", "Eve");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<User>(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasDiscount()
{
    var user = new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m);
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<PremiumUser>(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(((PremiumUser)loaded).DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Permanent hidden gap — one branch is never tested, yet the test always shows green.

---

### 🔴 HIGH 3 — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**Affected:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113-119)

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← no assertion
}
```

This test exercises code but verifies nothing. It will pass even if `Insert` silently discards every row. The name suggests a "does not throw" intent, but MSTest won't catch subtle data-loss bugs without an explicit assertion.

**Fix — assert the expected outcome:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    const int count = 100;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(count, users.Count,
        "All bulk-inserted users should be persisted.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — 100 % pass rate even if the repository is broken.

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette

**Affected:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35-38)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

Four assertions with **no explanation messages**. When one fails in CI, the log shows a generic `Assert.AreEqual failed` with no indication of *which* property was wrong without reading the code.

**Fix — add message parameters:**

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip correctly.");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip correctly.");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Persisted user should have a positive Id.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower CI triage — developers must open source to interpret failures.

---

## Smells Scanned — No Findings (15 of 19 clean)

| Smell | Verdict | Notes |
|-------|---------|-------|
| Mystery Guest | ✅ Clean | Uses in-memory SQLite — a hermetic test resource, appropriate for integration scope |
| Eager Test | ✅ Clean | No method calls 4+ distinct production methods; multi-step arrange is normal for integration tests |
| Magic Number Test | ✅ Clean | `3` in `ListUsers` and `10` in `ConcurrentInserts` are contextually obvious (items just added) |
| Sensitive Equality | ✅ Clean | No `ToString()` comparisons |
| Exception Handling | ✅ Clean | No `try`/`catch` blocks |
| General Fixture | ✅ Clean | Both `_connection` and `_repository` are used by all 8 tests |
| Ignored/Disabled Test | ✅ Clean | No `[Ignore]` attributes |
| Duplicate Assert | ✅ Clean | No duplicated assertion statements |
| Lazy Test | ✅ Clean | Each test has a distinct primary action |
| Constructor Init | ✅ Clean | Uses `[TestInitialize]` properly |
| Default Test | ✅ Clean | Class is meaningfully named |
| Redundant Print | ✅ Clean | No `Console.WriteLine` or debug output |
| Redundant Assertion | ✅ Clean | No tautological assertions |
| Resource Optimism | ✅ Clean | In-memory database — no external resource assumptions |
| Empty Test | ✅ Clean | All methods have executable statements |

---

## Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Test | Effort | Why First |
|----------|-------|------|--------|-----------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` | 5 min | Prevents false passes — this test catches zero bugs today |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` | 10 min | Eliminates a permanently dead branch |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` | 10 min | Removes 3 s wall-clock cost and flakiness risk |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` | 2 min | QoL for CI triage — low effort, add messages |

**Bottom line:** 4 of 8 tests have smells. The 3 high-severity findings (`BulkInsert`, `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType`, `NotifyOnInsert`) are the ones that actually risk hiding bugs or introducing flakiness. The other 4 tests (`InsertUser`, `UpdateUser`, `DeleteUser`, `ListUsers`, `ConcurrentInserts`) are well-structured — clean arrange/act/assert with appropriate integration-test patterns.
